module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.an_artifact_consulted_as_current_is_a_superseded_revision

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data an_artifact_consulted_as_current_is_a_superseded_revision: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "an_artifact_consulted_as_current_is_a_superseded_revision" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**an artifact consulted as current is a superseded revision** (a tool, a checkout, or any artifact is consulted as though it speaks for the corpus, and it speaks for an earlier revision of it. What it reports is confident, located, specific and internally consistent -- and wrong about the corpus as it now stands. Nothing in the reading says the artifact is old, so the report gets acted on: investigated, relayed, or issued as an instruction.)",

    "INVALID STATE: an artifact and the corpus disagree about which revision the artifact speaks for, with nothing that detects the disagreement, so the artifact is read as a statement about the corpus when it is a statement about a revision the corpus has left behind.",

    "WHY IT SURVIVES: the report is well-formed and of a kind the system genuinely produces, and in the second specimen it is a VERBATIM QUOTATION of a real authority. The reader's whole training is to trust a typed refusal and to trust a quoted authority -- correct for every case except this one. Nothing about the artifact announces its own age.",

    "FIRST SPECIMEN, AN ARTIFACT THAT EMITS A VERDICT (#11121, 2026-09-12). v2.std.collection moved map_insert/map_lookup onto host-realized primitive seams, changing BOTH sides in one commit: the .dag placeholder bodies and the Rust that realizes them (v1_interpreter.rs, v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated.rs, std_primitive_projection.rs) alongside dag/std/primitive_projection.dag. A gunbc binary built 2026-09-11 -- one day stale -- runs post-#11121 sources with a pre-#11121 realization set, so it EXECUTES a placeholder it was supposed to intercept and reports: 'call depth exceeded 100000 at fn map_lookup_primitive_delegate -- unbounded recursion (a bare-name resolution cycle, or a genuinely divergent chain)'. THE SELF-CALL IS BY DESIGN: dag/std/primitive_projection.dag marks the seam HostRealizedSeam, so a placeholder whose body calls itself is the CORRECT authored form and is never meant to run. No fact in the tree discriminates; the discriminator is the BINARY's build date against the seam's landing date, visible from neither the source nor the refusal. map_lookup and map_insert are std collection primitives, so an unknown but substantial share of .dag evaluation paths reach them; measured here, one witness diverged and one whole-roster projection did not. The hazard is not that every path fails -- it is that the failing and succeeding paths are indistinguishable in advance, and the succeeding ones emit plausible output. HARM RECEIPT: the author ran one targeted witness after a merge, received the above, and was approximately one message from reporting 'main has unbounded recursion in v2.std.collection' -- during a fleet-wide landing freeze protecting #10940's critical path, where a false alarm against main would have been acted on. Caught only by reading the seam's own projection row before reporting.",

    "SECOND SPECIMEN, AN ARTIFACT THAT IS THE TEXT (bright-boar-435, 2026-09-12). A session worktree sat at 683028dca3c while main was 7f6f22ceb52 -- 135 commits behind. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode was read from that tree, where `authored` is fold_list with concat and the annotation states 'each receipt carries its own trailing space, so this reproduces the single string it replaced BYTE FOR BYTE'. On main the same function is join(r.receipts, \" \") and its annotation records the OLD form as the hazard: 'an author dropping the space glued two receipts with nothing between them and nothing in the corpus refused'. On the strength of the superseded annotation a manager told a lane their new row was defective and instructed a worker to add a trailing space to every receipt -- a change that would have reintroduced the exact defect the replacement removed, into the file documenting this class. THIS IS THE HARDER CASE AND WHY THE CLASS IS NOT ABOUT TOOLS: THE ANNOTATION GENUINELY SAID WHAT IT WAS QUOTED AS SAYING. There was no misreading available to catch, and nothing emitted a verdict -- the stale artifact WAS the text being read. It was stopped only because the receiving lane checked the claim against origin/main instead of complying.",

    "THE BOUNDARY, because two rows in this directory now share the word 'superseded' and a reader who arrived by grepping it needs to know within one paragraph which they want. THIS row is the reader holding an OLD REVISION of a live authority whose current text is correct: nothing in the tree is wrong, the pointer resolves, and only the reading is stale. a_live_authority_name_carries_a_superseded_claim is the inverse: the tree itself carries a REFRESHED POINTER wrapped around a dead proposition, so the citation is current and the sentence around it is false. positional_citation and stable_citation_mutable_referent are a third thing again -- a POINTER DECAYING underneath a claim that stayed true. If the corpus is in good order and your copy of it is not, you are here.",

    "RUNG: found BELOW the ladder. Not a silent wrong answer but its twin -- a LOUD wrong answer, a fabricated plausible report (DESIGN section 5). In the first specimen the run fails closed in form and fails open in fact: it refuses, names a cause, locates it, and the cause is false. In the second the reading is a faithful quotation of a real authority and is false about the corpus. Nothing anywhere detects or bounds either today, so it is not even mitigated.",

    "CEILING 3, structurally guaranteed, and NOT 4. The mismatch cannot be made unwritable: artifacts consulted as current -- a host binary, a checkout, a cache, an index -- live outside the tree, and an old one can always exist, so no canonical model forbids the state. But it is DECIDABLE: a corpus can declare the revision it requires of what consults it, an artifact can carry the revision it speaks for, and the comparison is total. So it is reachable that no accepted CONSULTATION contains the state.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, named as a CAPABILITY and stated to cover BOTH kinds, because a trigger written for one will be satisfied while the other stays dead: EVERY ARTIFACT CONSULTED AS CURRENT CARRIES THE REVISION IT SPEAKS FOR, AND A CONSULTATION WHOSE REVISION DISAGREES WITH THE CORPUS REFUSES -- sufficient for an artifact that EMITS A VERDICT (a binary, a checker, an emitter, which can declare its revision and refuse) AND for one that IS THE TEXT (a checkout, a mirror, a cached copy, where nothing is emitted and the declaring party is the reader's own consultation). Rebuilding one binary or refreshing one checkout is NOT this trigger: each resolves a single instance and leaves the class intact, because the next seam to land, or the next commit to main, re-creates it for every artifact older than that landing.",

    "THE REMEDY AVAILABLE TODAY, which is mitigation and NOT the climb, stated separately so it is not mistaken for the trigger and does not read as discharging this row: read authorities with `git show origin/main:<path>` rather than from a working tree, and check `git rev-list --count HEAD..origin/main` before trusting a worktree read. For a tool, establish its build date against the landing date of whatever it names.",

    "REVIEW TELL, IN TWO HALVES, because a single tell covers only one of the two kinds and the reader half is the one that has bitten hardest. EMITTER HALF: a diagnostic naming unbounded recursion, a missing primitive, or an impossible construction, in code that reads as deliberate and that nobody recently changed -- establish the tool's build date against the landing date of whatever it names before investigating the code. READER HALF: a confident, VERBATIM citation of an authority, especially one that contradicts something you have measured. The quotation is usually accurate, which is what makes it persuasive; the question is not 'is this quoted correctly' but 'AT WHICH REVISION WAS IT READ'. This half needs no suspicion of error to trigger -- in the specimen above the quotation was faithful and the comment existed. BOTH HALVES IN ONE LINE: when a claim and your own measurement disagree, establish the REVISION each was read at before deciding which is wrong -- for a tool that is its build, for a file it is the checkout. Sharpest when you are about to tell someone else their work is wrong.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
